Z||000000||NASAReeves||05-16-2001||10:12 AM||||My '88 GT (5-Speed) clutch is slipping in 3rd+, and was rebuilt about two years ago.  I haven't really been satisfied with it...but who knows, maybe it's the driver??  Anyhow, I'll have to replace it in a few months, I'm sure.<P>I'm looking for something that won't be wimpy & slip on me after just a couple of years of normal driving.  Normal to me: We don't dog the car, but when I want to go...I want to go!  If I want to occasionally blow something off of the street, I want to be able to do that without the clutch being an obstacle.  BTW...The engine is the stock V6...most everything in my car is original, except the speakers, receiver, and the vacuum hoses (Rodney's steel).  She has 110k miles on her, but the compression is still fine.  I expect a rebuild in about 50k miles??<P>What should I look at in terms of a clutch?  I see the Fiero Store offers 3 options:  Stock (Yuck?), Heavy Duty, & Kevlar.  Money isn't a problem...I just want it to be 100% right, so I don't have to do this again 2 years down the road.  What is the 'normal' lifespan of a clutch?  What kit would you recommend? Anything else that should be replaced while I'm in there?  I am leaning toward the Kevlar, but I notice it says something about clutch "chatter."  What the heck is that?? (Honest...I don't know).<P>Thanks!!!<P>------------------<BR>Scott & Traci<BR>'88 GT<p>[This message has been edited by NASAReeves (edited 05-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||05-16-2001||11:48 AM||||Well, I'm using the Ram HD clutch from the Fiero Store and it seems ok.  I can't really tell if it's any better than a factory replacement.  I was thinking about the Kevlar, but the chatter turned me away.  In retrospect, I might have gone with it anyway. I've heard from other sources it's not that bad.  Chatter is when the clutch won't smoothly transition from engaged to disengaged and grabs a bit rough as you release the clutch.  You don't really notice it on a fast release - and since most of these are for racing or hi-perf use, that's not a concern there.  You also might try the Centerforce clutch V8 Archie suggests.  If it's good for a V8, it should be more than enough for a stock 2.8.  While you're in there, I'd definately consider replacing the input shaft bearing, since it appears to be a weak link and if it goes, it'll take the clutch with it.  You have to split the case, but get Rodney's instructions.  It's very easy.  I had never done it before, but have no reservations about doing it again.  I was actually kicking myself for not doing it sooner once I realized how easy it was.<P>There's lots of clutches out there and most everyone here will recommend something different.  Try to get info from people who actually HAVE the clutch they're recommending.  Too many recommendations are heresay.  I'm satisfied with my Ram, but next time I may go with the racing clutch.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||NASAReeves||05-16-2001||12:29 PM||||Thanks, Formula.  I'm considering the kevlar...but as you said...I want to hear what people think about what they have.  <P>I'm also considering the Aluminum Flywheel, but that seems to be a bit costly (more than the clutch).  Then again, I'm thinking of doing the work myself, and to-date, the most in-depth thing I've done is pull the upper plenum to replace a few sensors, vacuum lines, and EGR Tube.  If I have to take my car apart, I only want to have to do it once.  I might go for the flywheel, too.  <P>Everybody...tell me about the clutches you are running.  I've heard about the 4 & 6-puck, but I don't want to kill my drivetrain.  I guess if I don't race it a lot...it won't really kill it though??<P>Thanks for the tip about the 'weak link.'  Any other advise?  ||||reg||1
